  "query_preview": "..\n\nA quick moving and weak upper level shortwave trough continues to\npush through the area today, which is resulting in a breezy day \nacross North Central Montana, with Southwestern Montana seeing a \ncalmer day today. This system will exit the area later this \nafternoon, after which upper level ridging will quickly build \nacross the Rockies and up into Central Canada, which will result \nin a short-lived omega block over much of Canada and the United \nStates. On the surface, expect temperatures to soar to 15-20 \ndegrees above climatological norms for mid-November Wednesday \nthrough Friday, with slightly cooler, but still above average, \ntemperatures over the weekend when the ridge axis shifts a bit \nfurther eastward. \n\nAs we head into the weekend and beyond, a series of strong upper\nlevel lows will move across the Pacific and into the West Coast.\nWhile how exactly this plays out is a bit uncertain, but overall\nexpect a period of unsettled weather pattern with multiple\nopportunities for mountain snow and valley rain or snow as\nmultiple systems embedded in the upper level flow push onto the\nwest coast. Looking further into the middle of next week, the\noverall trend is for some version of a cooler pattern as cold air\nsurges southward. Ludwig",
